---
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Sources & credits — Canal Mania and the Philosophers Stone</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Sources and credits for the Canal Mania and the Philosophers Stone knowledge base." />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fraunces:ital,opsz,wght@0,9..144,400;0,9..144,600;1,9..144,400&family=Source+Sans+3:ital,wght@0,400;0,600;1,400&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <header>
        <h1>Sources <span class="amp">&</span> credits</h1>
        <p class="back"><a href={`${base}/`}>← back to the conversation</a></p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <p>
          This research draws on 16 curated sources spanning two strands. The first strand — the
          Industrial Revolution itself — rests on eleven works ranging from foundational economic
          histories (Ashton's <em>The Industrial Revolution 1760–1830</em>, Landes's
          <em>The Unbound Prometheus</em>, Hobsbawm's <em>Industry and Empire</em>) to modern
          reinterpretations of why Britain industrialized first (Allen's
          <em>The British Industrial Revolution in Global Perspective</em>, Mokyr's
          <em>The Enlightened Economy</em>), social "history from below" (E. P. Thompson's
          <em>The Making of the English Working Class</em>, Griffin's <em>Liberty's Dawn</em>),
          narrative and popular history (Uglow's <em>The Lunar Men</em>, the
          <a href="https://www.industrialrevolutionspod.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >Industrial Revolutions podcast</a
          >), and vetted reference and primary-source material (Encyclopædia Britannica plus a set
          of digital archives including the Smithsonian and British History Online).
        </p>
        <p>
          The second strand asks how today's AI wave compares. It draws on peer-reviewed economics
          and expert commentary that frame AI as a general-purpose technology like steam or
          electricity — Crafts's historical-perspective article, Brynjolfsson, Rock &amp;
          Syverson's "Productivity J-Curve," Veldkamp &amp; Abis's work summarized by Columbia
          Business School, a VoxDev conversation with economic historian Bruno Caprettini, and an
          AEI piece on the AI–electricity parallel.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Built with</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Sanity Knowledge Bases and Context</li>
          <li>Anthropic (Haiku)</li>
          <li>Astro</li>
          <li>Netlify</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="notice">
          This site is only for educational and entertainment purposes. No commercial use of this
          knowledge base is permitted. Any reference should be quoted back to the original
          sources.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <style>
      :root {
        --paper: #f4efe5;
        --paper-deep: #ece4d4;
        --ink: #2b2620;
        --ink-soft: #6b6154;
        --historian: #8a4a24;
        --technologist: #1f6f78;
      }

      * {
        box-sizing: border-box;
      }

      html,
      body {
        margin: 0;
      }

      body {
        background: var(--paper);
        color: var(--ink);
        font-family: 'Source Sans 3', system-ui, sans-serif;
        display: flex;
        justify-content: center;
      }

      main {
        width: min(720px, 100%);
        padding: 1.5rem 1.25rem 3rem;
      }

      header {
        border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
        padding-bottom: 0.8rem;
        margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
      }

      h1 {
        font-family: 'Fraunces', Georgia, serif;
        font-weight: 600;
        font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 4vw, 2rem);
        margin: 0;
      }

      h2 {
        font-family: 'Fraunces', Georgia, serif;
        font-weight: 600;
        font-size: 1.1rem;
        margin: 1.6rem 0 0.4rem;
      }

      .amp {
        font-style: italic;
        color: var(--historian);
      }

      .back {
        margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
        font-size: 0.85rem;
      }

      a {
        color: var(--technologist);
      }

      p {
        line-height: 1.6;
      }

      ul {
        line-height: 1.7;
        padding-left: 1.3rem;
      }

      .notice {
        border-top: 1px solid var(--paper-deep);
        padding-top: 1rem;
        font-size: 0.88rem;
        color: var(--ink-soft);
        font-style: italic;
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
